A||||6||Wannabe||Removing Fastback Section....HELP!||||11||
Z||000000||Wannabe||07-11-2000||11:07 PM||||Isn't always the very last bolt! While removing the fasteners to be able to remove the rear clip, I came to one of the bolts by the sunroof opening. I began to turn it, and without applying more torque than was required to loosen the other bolts, I heard a  cracking sound. Now the bolt is spinning freely without coming loose. I suspect that whatever insert the bolt threads into has broken from the plastic body panel and is now just loose inside. Before I drill the #%*&@ thing out and motor on with one less bolt holding the roof panel in place, does anyone have any suggestions?||||reg||11
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||07-12-2000||12:17 AM||||I had a similar problem when I removed my notchback clip and ended up destroying it. I didn't want it anymore and I got pretty steamed. I don't know of a way to get that bolt free... just be careful and good luck.<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR>V6 Fastback||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||07-12-2000||12:46 AM||||If it spins you might not be able to drill it. If it is one of the recessed ones. If it is exposed you could hold it with vise grips and then drill it or just grind it. If it is recessed you might have to pry the roof a little while you turn the bolt it might come out.  If all else fails, Hopefully you can break the boss holding it before you break the roof.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wannabe||07-12-2000||01:12 AM||||Oh man, these alternatives don't sound too promising....time to get the drill out. The bolt head is exposed, so I can hold it in place with a wrench while the head is being drilled. Are there any more surprises I need to know about before I try getting the thing off? ||||reg||11
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-12-2000||07:46 AM||||Call Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory.  I'm sure he can help out with info.<P>(256) 420-5391<BR>Fierofactory@juno.com||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||07-12-2000||09:51 PM||||The nuts are on a "bolt" that has a "T" on the end and is molded in the clip. I tried to lift up the rear of the clip to put pressure on it hoping it would catch enough to get the nut off... didn't work.<P>Not sure if you can drill that bolt to free the nut. Not sure you can even grind it off since the nut is recessed inside the roof section of the chassis.<P>I am full of all kinds of good news aren't I?<P>Just curious... why you taking the clip off?<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR>V6 Fastback||||reg||1
Z||000006||RLMay||07-13-2000||11:47 PM||||I'm in the process of repainting my 86 GT and decided to remove the body to do so.  I currently have my parts car body mounted.  I experienced the same problem in removing one of the bost to the rear section (fastback).  I tried prying up on the pannel, but it did no good.  I finally decided to cut a hole in the top with my dremel tool.  I then could grab the top of the bolt with locking plyers and the nut was easily removed.  I ten rebuild the poket area of the top where the bolt head was supposed to be held in place and and shaped it with my dremel tool.  Note that the bolts are a little loose, I supposed for alignment purposes when installing the pannel.  I placed some was paper inthe hole between the two sections of the pannel to allow for some movement of the bolt and rebuilt the hole in the top with fiber glass.  I havenet got it reinstalled yet, so I can't attest as to how well it will tighten down, but I'm hopeful||||reg||1
